<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  CI run settings: the same unit tests, executed on the nanoclr *virtual device*
  instead of a physical board. (No count here: it was stale within three commits of
  being written, and the run reports its own.)

  This is a second file rather than a switch inside nano.runsettings because the settings
  are declarative and a reviewer should be able to see, in one place, exactly what CI
  runs. The local default (nano.runsettings) still targets real hardware, so running
  .\scripts\Run-Tests.ps1 with no arguments behaves as it always has.

  Pass it with:  .\scripts\Run-Tests.ps1 -RunSettings nano.ci.runsettings

  Worth knowing: the virtual device runs the same managed IL through the same CLR, so it
  catches everything except behaviour that depends on the physical target, meaning WiFi,
  I2C, and timing against real peripherals. Those live in src\integrationTests and cannot
  run here at all.

  (No double hyphens anywhere in this file: XML forbids them inside comments, and
  vstest.console rejects the whole settings file rather than ignoring the comment.)
-->
<RunSettings>
   <RunConfiguration>
       <ResultsDirectory>.\TestResults</ResultsDirectory>
       <!-- Generous next to the ~18s a local hardware run takes: a cold CI agent pays for
            the first nanoclr start, and a timeout here is indistinguishable from a hang,
            which is a miserable thing to debug from a log. -->
       <TestSessionTimeout>300000</TestSessionTimeout>
       <TargetFrameworkVersion>net48</TargetFrameworkVersion>
       <TargetPlatform>x64</TargetPlatform>
   </RunConfiguration>
   <nanoFrameworkAdapter>
       <Logging>Detailed</Logging>
       <IsRealHardware>False</IsRealHardware>
       <RealHardwarePort></RealHardwarePort>
       <!-- The CLR these tests run on. This is the one place it is declared, and
            Run-Tests.ps1 reads the value from here.

            It used to be empty, with a comment saying the workflow's own pin on the
            nanoclr global tool decided the runtime. That was never true: the test adapter
            replaces that tool with the latest published one before every virtual-device
            run, naming no version, and then has it fetch the latest CLR. CI installs
            1.1.122 and the run reports 1.1.311; locally, one run moved this machine's
            tool from 1.1.289 to 1.1.311 on its own.

            Which cost nothing until the latest CLR stopped matching the managed
            baseline. On 2026-09-21 main went red with "Firmware version does not match
            managed code version" and 1 of 170 tests executed: CoreLibrary 1.17.11, which
            every project here references, is accepted by CLR 1.17.0.316 and by the
            1.17.0.339 on the ESP32, and refused by 1.17.0.349 and by 1.18.0.18. Nothing
            in the tree changed that day. Issue #162 carries the diagnosis.

            1.17.0.316 is the last CLR CI ran green on with these packages, which is where
            the number comes from. Moving the managed baseline to 1.18 instead is a
            managed and firmware migration together, and not something a test run should
            decide by downloading whatever shipped this morning.

            PathToLocalCLRInstance is left empty on purpose: it is a machine path, and
            Run-Tests.ps1 fills it into a resolved copy of this file per run, the way
            Deploy-DeviceConfig.ps1 resolves nanoff's manifest. Setting CLRVersion alone
            does not hold, because the adapter asks the tool for it under an option name
            the current CLI does not have. -->
       <CLRVersion>1.17.0.316</CLRVersion>
       <PathToLocalCLRInstance></PathToLocalCLRInstance>
   </nanoFrameworkAdapter>
</RunSettings>
